WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
A stroke occurs when blood stops flowing to a part of the brain because a blood vessel

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.580
is either blocked or has a leak.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:12.260
Just like the heart, the brain needs a constant supply of oxygen to survive, and when that

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.059
flow is interrupted, every second counts.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:21.299
To help you identify a stroke quickly, we use the acronym BFAST.

00:00:21.299 --> 00:00:23.299
B stands for balance.

00:00:23.299 --> 00:00:27.260
Watch if the person is suddenly stumbling or having trouble standing straight.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.260
E is for eyes.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.259
Ask them if their vision is suddenly blurry or if they are seeing double.

00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:35.980
F for face drooping.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:41.980
Usually, a stroke only affects one side of the body, so ask the person to smile for you.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:47.099
If one side of their mouth stays down while the other goes up, that is a major red flag.

00:00:47.099 --> 00:00:48.820
A is for arm weakness.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.740
You can ask them to raise both arms out straight.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.459
If one arm drifts downward or they can't lift it at all, they may be having a stroke.

00:00:56.459 --> 00:01:00.180
Another great check is to have them squeeze your hands to see if their grip strength is

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.860
the same on both sides.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.019
S stands for slurred speech, which happens when the brain is struggling to control the

00:01:06.019 --> 00:01:08.040
muscles used for talking.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.260
That leads us to T, which stands for time to call 911.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:16.580
Even showing just one of these signs is enough to justify an emergency call.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:20.660
If possible, try to note the exact time the symptoms started.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.639
This helps doctors at the hospital decide which specific treatments are safe to give.

00:01:25.639 --> 00:01:29.959
After you've called for help, simply wait with the person until EMS arrives and stay

00:01:29.959 --> 00:01:34.080
prepared to start CPR if they become unresponsive or stop breathing.

